<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Probability</title>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's begin with the function <md>p(x) =
  \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-x^2/2},</md>
  which defines a ``normal distribution.''
  Plot this function and verify that it has the
  shape of a bell curve centered about the point <m>x=0</m>.</p>
        <p>One inconvenience in dealing with this function is that it does not
  have an antiderivative in terms of functions that are familiar to
  us.  Therefore, any definite integrals that we would like to
  evaluate involving this function require the use of a numerical
  method.  That means that you'll need to use Desmos to evaluate any
  definite integrals of this function.</p>
        <p>Since this is a probability density function, what should be the value of 
  <md>\int_{-\infty}^\infty p(x)~dx?</md>  Verify that this is the case.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the probability that a data point will lie between <m>0</m> and
  <m>1</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that we measure the heights of men in the United States and
  find the normal distribution
  <md>
  p(x)=\frac1{3\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-(x-70)^2/18}</md>
  where <m>x</m> is measured in inches.</p>
        <p>Plot this function (what is a reasonable range of <m>x</m>
  values?) and discuss the significance of the graph.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the average height.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Then find the fraction of men whose height is between 70 and 72
  inches.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What fraction of men are taller than 74 inches?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the median height?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>\overline{x}</m> is the mean of the density function, the standard
  deviation <m>\sigma</m>, which measures the spread in the data, is given
  by
  <md>\sigma^2 = \int_{-\infty}^\infty (x-\overline{x})^2p(x)~dx.
  </md>
  Notice that this is defining <m>\sigma^2</m> so you'll need to take the
  square root of the integral.  Find the standard deviation of this
  distribution.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The general form of the normal distribution is
  <md>
  p(x) = \frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-\frac{(x-\bar{x})^2}{2\sigma^2}}.
  </md>
  Average annual snowfall in Grand Rapids is 72.4 inches with a standard
  deviation of 16 inches.  There was a lot of snow last year with 109
  inches recorded.  What is the chance that there is more than 109
  inches of snow?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>So far this year, we've had about 40 inches of snow.  What is the
  chance that there is 40 or fewer inches of snow in a year?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the chance that the snowfall is more than the average?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the chance that the snowfall is more than one standard
  deviation above the average?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
